● Yes, technically it can — but it doesn't cleanly replace the CTest test,
  mainly because of a Windows ordering issue. Here's the honest assessment.

  How it would work as a CppUnit case

  A CppUnit test can't run the GUI in-process (it needs a separate process with
  a Qt event loop + offscreen platform), but it can launch the GUI binary as a 
  subprocess and assert a clean exit:

  void GuiStartupTest::testStartup() {
      QProcess p;
      auto env = QProcessEnvironment::systemEnvironment();
      env.insert( "QT_QPA_PLATFORM", "offscreen" );
      env.insert( "HOME", <temp dir> );
      p.setProcessEnvironment( env );
      p.start( H2_GUI_EXECUTABLE,                       // path from CMake
               { "--nosplash", "--child", "--quit-after-startup", "-P", <data> }
  );
      CPPUNIT_ASSERT( p.waitForFinished( 120000 ) );
      CPPUNIT_ASSERT_EQUAL( 0, p.exitCode() );
  }
  The binary path comes from CMake via a compile define:
  target_compile_definitions(tests PRIVATE 
  H2_GUI_EXECUTABLE="$<TARGET_FILE:hydrogen>") (the generator expression
  resolves the .app/.exe correctly and works even though src/tests is configured
  before src/gui), plus add_dependencies(tests hydrogen). It registers in
  registeredTests.h like any other case and shows up in the OK (N tests) count.

  The trade-offs (why I went with CTest)

  1. Windows deploy ordering — the real blocker. In the AppVeyor Windows job,
  src\tests\tests.exe runs before windeployqt deploys Qt. So the built
  hydrogen.exe has no Qt platform plugins yet → a CppUnit subprocess launching
  it would fail to start. That's exactly why the CTest path runs the installed
  binary after install. A CppUnit case would have to skip on Windows, so you'd
  still need the CTest/installed step for Windows coverage — i.e., you'd keep
  both.
  2. Coupling. It couples the unit-test binary to the GUI binary (path + build
  order). Minor here (the GUI is always built — add_subdirectory(src/gui) is
  unconditional), but it inverts the current independence of the unit suite from
  the GUI.
  3. Concept. It's a process-level integration test living inside the in-process
  unit suite — CTest is the more idiomatic home for "run this binary, check the
  exit code."

  Recommendation

  Keep the smoke test in CTest (its natural layer, and the only thing that
  handles the Windows installed-binary case), which build.sh t already runs.
  Duplicating it into CppUnit doesn't remove the Windows special-case, so you'd
  carry both.

  That said, if your goal is specifically "it should appear in the CppUnit OK (N
  tests) report and run through the existing src/tests/tests invocation on 
  Linux/macOS," I can add the QProcess case with a Windows skip (Windows keeps
  the installed-binary CTest step).

