  What "finish T1.5" literally means

  T1.5 = delete the transitional registry: __instance + setInstance() +
  get_instance() + create_instance(). The proof-of-done is "the build fails on a
  lingering get_instance()."

  Why it's all-or-nothing (and currently load-bearing)

  Hydrogen.cpp:124/127 register the instance as the process-current via
  Preferences::setInstance() / EventQueue::setInstance(). That registry is still
  actively depended on by:

  1. ~13 guarded fallbacks in core (load-tree, Transport, Filesystem, Song…) —
  the if(null) get_instance() safety nets.
  2. Static-context callers with no this to thread from — confirmed by the
  in-code comments at AudioEngine.cpp:3241 and JackDriver.cpp:2142 ("…falls back
  to get_instance"). getLeadLagInFrames and the Jack/timebase callbacks are
  static.
  3. The GUI null-safe accessors (HydrogenApp::pPreferences() falls back to the
  singleton during construction — the segfault fix).
  4. AudioEngineTests (28) — test code compiled into core.
  5. The instance-mechanism tests themselves — EventQueueTest (19),
  PreferencesInstanceTest (7), MultiInstanceTest (4) exist to test
  get_instance/setInstance.

  You can't delete setInstance while any fallback calls get_instance — so this
  can't land incrementally and stay green. It also pulls in work that belongs to
  P3 (the process-callback void* arg for the static audio path), T1.6
  (AE_*LOG/assertLocked still resolve the driver-name prefix via get_instance),
  an AudioEngineTests restructure, and rewriting the three instance-mechanism 
  tests to construct instances directly instead of asserting on the registry.

  That collides with two standing constraints: green at every step and no 
  commits. So the scope is a real decision, not something I should pick
  unilaterally.

