● Fixed.

  Root cause: my own G6 bootstrap static

  The leak was introduced by the GUI bootstrap mechanism I added in T1.5.
  HydrogenApp::m_pBootstrapPreferences is a static std::shared_ptr<Preferences>
  (set by setBootstrap() so early-startup code like Skin::setPalette can reach
  Preferences before HydrogenApp exists). Being a never-reset static shared_ptr,
  it pinned the entire Preferences object graph forever — 16 objects:
  Preferences + Theme/ColorTheme/InterfaceTheme/FontTheme + MidiEventMap +
  MidiInstrumentMap + Shortcuts + 8 WindowProperties.

  One clarification on "Logger"

  The objects map reported by write_objects_map_to_cerr() doesn't actually track
  Logger — Logger is a plain class Logger {…}, not an Object subclass, so it
  never appears there (and count_active() ignores it). What the map did show
  leaking was the Preferences graph above, and Hydrogen itself destructed
  cleanly. The per-instance Logger (owned by Hydrogen::m_pLogger, a shared_ptr)
  and the default logger (delete Logger::get_instance() in main) are both freed
  correctly.

  The fix

  ~HydrogenApp now resets both bootstrap statics:
  m_pBootstrapHydrogen = nullptr;       // would dangle after delete m_pHydrogen
  m_pBootstrapPreferences = nullptr;    // dropped the ref pinning the 
  Preferences graph
  The destructor runs (via delete pMainForm) before main()'s count_active()
  check, and combined with main's pPref = nullptr, the last reference is
  released.

  Verification

  - GUI startup+teardown objects map: Total: 0 objects alive (was 16); every
  class row balances constr==destr.
  - OK (225 tests) + GuiStartup smoke passes.

