  What changed

  OscServer / NsmClient are no longer singletons. Each Hydrogen owns them as
  members (m_pOscServer / m_pNsmClient), created in the ctor and freed in the
  dtor, exposed via getOscServer() / getNsmClient() — exactly like the other
  hubs (AudioEngine, CoreActionController, …). Removed: __instance,
  get_instance(), create_instance(), setInstance(), and the static m_pHydrogen
  back-pointers. All callers (CoreActionController ×7, JackDriver, MainForm,
  PreferencesDialog, DrumkitPropertiesDialog, gui main, the standalone/test
  mains) now go through the per-instance accessors.

  The bManageGlobalOsc workaround is gone — with OSC/NSM owned per-instance
  there's no shared singleton to contend for, so the multi-instance tests just
  construct plain Hydrogens, each with its own OSC server / NSM client. Only one
  binds an actual OSC port at a time, which is a runtime config concern
  (getOscServerEnabled/port), not shared ownership.

  The liblo C-callback constraint (the reason these stayed singletons) was
  handled by:
  - making m_pHydrogen and the ~80 OSC handlers non-static;
  - a one-place addMethod(path, types, &OscServer::X_Handler) helper that binds
  each per-message handler to its instance via a this-capturing lambda;
  - routing the catch-all generic_handler and the NSM OpenCallback/SaveCallback
  (which must stay static C-callbacks) to their instance through liblo/NSM
  user_data (this).

  The debugging note worth recording

  This cost real time: OscServer and NsmClient are declared in the global 
  namespace (their namespace H2Core {…} block closes before the class — the
  giveaway is public H2Core::Object<…>). My initial forward declarations inside
  namespace H2Core created phantom, never-defined
  H2Core::OscServer/H2Core::NsmClient types, which surfaced as "incomplete type"
  everywhere. Fix: forward-declare them at global scope. (An in-build #error
  probe is what finally pinned it down, after a corrupted-flags
  isolated-preprocess sent me chasing a phantom include-order issue.)

